    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1035">

                <pb facs="0959" n="947" xml:id="pg947"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.6354/187: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-11T13:00:00+00:00">December 11,
                            1939—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 11—8:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2578. Embassy’s 2553, December 6, 6 p.m.<anchor n="95" xml:id="d1035-f1"/>
                    Colonial Office announces today that the Government of the Straits Settlements
                    has cancelled the order made on September 18 last, prescribing a maximum price
                    for tin from that colony. Cancellation takes effect forthwith.</p>
                <p>In pursuance of regulations 55 and 98 of the Defense Regulations 1939, the
                    Ministry of Supply has issued control of tin (No. 2) order, 1939, dated December
                    8, revoking with effect from December 11, 1939, control of tin (No. 1) order of
                    September 17, 1939. The latter order fixed the maximum price for tin on the
                    basis of £230 a ton for standard tin and the new order removes this
                    restriction.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
